ediation epic. |\n40\t\n41\tNo other edges: the remediation epic is a single logical change (see HLR Traceability)\n42\tand there are no external dependencies, investigations, or onboarding chains — the\n43\tDashboard is an existing, Konflux-onboarded component per architecture context.\n44\t\n45\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n46\t\n47\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n48\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n49\t| HLR-1 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Keyboard operability of the four audited flows. |\n50\t| HLR-2 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | `bundled` — consistent focus indicators edit the same flows, components, and UI surfaces as the HLR-1 focus-trap/unreachable-control fixes (same modal dialogs, same forms, same CSS/patternfly surface). Splitting HLR-2 into its own epic would double-touch the same files and produce two epics building the same surface, so it stays bundled with HLR-1. Both are P1, so no deferral signal is lost. |\n51\t| HLR-3 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | VPAT and accessibility statement update, technical review, and republish. |\n52\t\n53\tAll HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n54\t\n55\t## Health Warnings\n56\t\n57\t- **Missing strategy sections**: The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n58\t  Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), and no Non-Functional\n59\t  Requirements section. Epic acceptance criteria below were derived from the HLRs and\n60\t  the problem statement; the strategy should ideally be back-filled with explicit ACs.\n61\t- **Team inference**: The strategy does not state owners. The Dashboard epic is\n62\t  assigned to RHOAI Dashboard per the architecture context file\n63\t  (`architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`, owning team RHOAI Dashboard). The\n64\t  documentation epic is assigned to RHOAI Documentation as the Red Hat docs content\n65\t  process owner; the technical review AC routes Dashboard engineering sign-off. Verify\n66\t  both assignments with the strategy author.\n67\t- **Scope tr